Write each position as $vb(x_i) = vb(R) + vb(y_i)$, where $vb(y_i)$ is the position of the $i$-th particle relative to the centre of mass. Then $sum_i m_i vb(y_i) = vb(0)$, and since this holds for all time, $sum_i m_i vb(dot(y)_i) = vb(0)$. The kinetic energy therefore splits as
$
  T = 1/2 M abs(vb(dot(R)))^2 + underbracket(sum_i 1/2 m_i abs(vb(dot(y)_i))^2, "internal kinetic energy"),
$
a centre-of-mass kinetic energy plus an internal kinetic energy.
